<%# locals: (label: "Proceed to Checkout", button_class: "btn btn-primary w-full") %>
<%# Shared checkout button that fires the GA4 begin_checkout dataLayer event on submit,
    mirroring the cart-page form (cart_items/_checkout.html.erb). The form-level analytics
    wiring comes from analytics_checkout_form_data, the single home for that contract
    (it also gates the cost on gtm_enabled?).

    Rendered by the cart drawer. It was also rendered by the navbar cart dropdown until
    that was removed; the partial stays shared because the drawer appears on nine pages
    via shared/_cart_drawer.

    turbo: false keeps this a full navigation to the Stripe-bound checkout_path; the
    dataLayer push happens on submit, before the redirect.

    Strict locals (defaults above) raise a clear error rather than 500-ing on a missing
    local, and give callers sensible defaults.

    Self-guard so the partial is safe in isolation (callers already guard, but a nil/empty
    Current.cart would otherwise crash on total_amount). No cart, no checkout button. Uses
    the memoized line_items_count rather than cart_items.any? to avoid an extra SELECT 1. %>
<% return unless (Current.cart&.line_items_count || 0).positive? %>
<%# Checkout is refused without a delivery destination it can price (shipping is
    baked into the Stripe line items before Stripe collects the address), so with
    no destination this disables the button and says what is missing rather than
    offering a POST into a guaranteed rejection. The condition matches
    CheckoutsController#deliverable_destination? (see the note in
    cart_items/_checkout.html.erb on why they must agree).

    Every surface that offers checkout now carries a postcode field, so this can
    always name the fix in place. There used to be a third branch linking to
    /cart, for the navbar dropdown that had no field of its own; it went with the
    dropdown, since a branch no caller can reach is a branch that cannot be
    trusted to still work. %>
<% if delivery_destination_known?(Current.cart, address_postcode: cart_owner_default_postcode(Current.cart)) %>
  <%= form_with url: checkout_path, method: :post, data: analytics_checkout_form_data(Current.cart) do %>
    <button type="submit" class="<%= button_class %>"><%= label %></button>
  <% end %>
<% else %>
  <%# No begin_checkout here, and that is deliberate: the customer cannot begin
      checkout yet, so reporting that they did would overstate the funnel. The
      cart page's disabled button behaves the same way. %>
  <button type="submit" class="<%= button_class %>" disabled><%= label %></button>
  <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-center text-gray-600" data-test="drawer-checkout-blocked-note">
    <%= checkout_blocked_note %>
  </p>
<% end %>
